  var ENO = {"homizy": {"sec": [{"h": "The problem", "t": "Homizy was born from a radical choice: believing in compatibility, not in square meters. People still search for homes by size, neighborhood and price, but co-living doesn't fail for lack of space. It fails for lack of alignment.", "sc": 1, "st": [["65%", "of people name usual schedules"], ["47%", "name habits"], ["43%", "name how needs are communicated"]], "pts": [["Where it really breaks", "The gap is never the square meters. It is the alignment between people.", ["Communication", "Rhythms", "Schedules", "Habits"]]], "q": "“Co-living doesn't fail because of the spaces.”"}, {"h": "The insights", "t": "User profiling and room matching were built on two kinds of evidence: qualitative tools (benchmarks and interviews) and quantitative ones (surveys). One need came out clearly: a system capable of turning the roommate from a risk into a tangible enrichment.", "sc": 1, "st": [["67%", "practical compatibility"], ["33%", "social compatibility"]]}, {"h": "The Solution: ZyMatch & ZyMate", "t": "Homizy puts people at the center and turns housing into a shared experience built on affinity. The solution has a dual nature, enhancing compatibility through two distinct flows: ZyMatch & ZyMate.", "pts": [["1 · Practical flow", "During onboarding, users complete a questionnaire that profiles their habits and lets them define the type of roommate they are looking for. Matching criteria are weighted by importance, and the system generates three personalized housing proposals showing overall and detailed compatibility percentages.", ["Schedules &amp; cleanliness 25%", "Language 20%", "Sociality &amp; age 10%", "Culture, hobbies &amp; interests 5%"]], ["2 · Social flow", "Four archetypes define identity and belonging, turning a shared flat into a social ecosystem. After the assignment, users can explore their house right away or come back to it later from their profile. On its dedicated page they discover the house's characteristics and its personalized events. For people who move for work: a ready-made community, less isolation, easier networking and a home that helps them switch off.", ["Methodical", "Dreamers", "Trustworthy", "Passionate"], "inl"]], "q": "“Putting compatibility at the center of the experience.”"}]}, "antianti": {"sec": [{"h": "The problem", "t": "In a landscape dominated by fast fashion and impulse buying, Generation Z doesn't like throwing away clothes they feel attached to, yet is used to replacing them the moment they get damaged.", "pts": [["The critical moment", "The instant a piece stops fitting the user's story."]], "q": "“In the 21st century, nothing is indispensable.”"}, {"h": "The insights", "t": "The issue is not lack of awareness. It's the value drop that happens when a garment gets damaged: in that moment the piece no longer supports the user's identity, and the easiest solution becomes buying something new.", "pts": [["The value drop", "A damaged garment stops expressing who the user is.", null, "tb"], ["The paradox", "Gen Z is very sensitive to sustainability, yet only sees the opportunity tied to consumption.", null, "tb"]], "q": "“Not a lack of awareness: a value drop.”"}, {"h": "The Solution: AntiAnti", "t": "A subscription-based digital service that intercepts this critical moment and turns it into an opportunity. Not a sewing tutorial, but a guided experience that helps users reinterpret and repair damaged clothes to make them desirable, and resellable, again.", "pts": [["Unlocking potential", "The value is not in perfection.<br>Inspired by the Japanese philosophy of Kintsugi, flaws become distinctive elements rather than defects to hide."], ["Gradual challenges", "Ideas, Personal and Sales sections let users take on gradual challenges, earn points and unlock more complex ones with high resale potential. It starts with accessible paths for heavily worn items like T-shirts."]], "q": "“It doesn't just teach how to fix clothes. It changes how value is perceived.”"}]}, "perii": {"sec": [{"h": "The problem", "t": "In Italy there are currently no dedicated digital solutions designed specifically for Alzheimer's patients, and even fewer addressing one of the most critical and dangerous symptoms: wandering.", "st": [["3M", "Alzheimer's caregivers in Italy, most of them related to the patient"]], "pts": [["A risk that can't be predicted", "Wandering is not just disorientation. It is an unpredictable behavior that exposes people with Alzheimer's to serious risks, and it leaves caregivers in a constant state of anxiety and hypervigilance: always alert, never able to relax, even when nothing is happening.", null, "tb"], ["A fear that stays", "It is a fear that stays: the thought of “what if they walk away?” often prevents families from leaving their loved ones alone, even for short periods of time. Even a quick errand becomes a source of worry.", null, "tb"]]}, {"h": "The insights", "t": "To understand the people behind the gap, the research combined interviews, desk research, field work and a benchmark. Caregivers are 62 years old on average and live wandering as a frequent thought, so the answer had to be simple and accessible.", "sc": 1, "st": [["8", "caregivers interviewed"], ["2", "neuropsychologists"], ["1", "care worker (OSS) in an Alzheimer's facility"], ["62", "average age of caregivers"]], "pts": [["How we listened", "Two interview scripts, one for caregivers and one for professionals, plus a full day in the field with a caregiver. With no Italian digital project to learn from, the benchmark looked abroad and at pet-tracking apps like Tractive.", ["Interviews", "Desk research", "Field research", "Benchmark"]]]}, {"h": "The Solution: PERII", "t": "PERII was designed to address this gap directly: a digital safety ecosystem that reduces the risks of wandering while supporting both patients and caregivers. At its core is a GPS-enabled wearable bracelet connected to a simple, accessible mobile app. The app is built around the caregiver: larger text and line spacing, simple concepts, attention to the thumb zone, a WhatsApp-like palette and a voice reader for those with vision problems.", "pts": [["Real-time location", "Caregivers can monitor the patient's location at any moment."], ["Safe zones", "Caregivers set predefined boundaries around places where the patient can move freely."], ["Instant alerts", "An alert arrives as soon as a boundary is crossed, with three choices: reach the person, call, or share their position with a relative."], ["A bracelet built around the patient", "An easy-to-change battery, in an AirTag-like system, avoids the stress of daily recharging. An elastic yet resistant material prevents easy removal."]], "q": "“PERII, where to find you again.”"}]}, "lumen": {"d": "An AI agent, built with Tonidigrigio, that turns scattered team information into clarity.", "sec": [{"h": "The problem", "po": 1, "t": "It's Monday morning. A project is already moving fast, but no one has a clear picture: ask two people and you get two different answers.", "pts": [["Which task should be prioritized?", "It depends on who you ask.", null, "flat"], ["Where's the latest client feedback?", "Somewhere between Slack, Drive, emails and meeting notes.", null, "flat"]], "st": [["67%", "of the team doesn't know how much budget remains on a project"], ["73%", "say initial estimates are exceeded by 30–50%"], ["56%", "experience shifting priorities at least three times a week"], ["40%", "of working documents never reach the entire team"]]}, {"h": "The insights", "t": "This isn't a people problem. It's an information problem that needs to be fixed. Where does the time go? Time is spent searching, aligning and catching up instead of creating value. That is why we need to requalify how we work: in 2026, work habits and environments need to be requalified to improve the quality of tasks, and the quality of the team's actions.", "q": "“It isn't a people problem. It's an information problem.”"}, {"h": "The Solution: Lumen", "t": "Now imagine an AI agent embedded directly into the team's workflow, built in collaboration with Tonidigrigio. Not another tool to learn, but a teammate, like a kind senior colleague: it connects information, tracks project health, surfaces risks and delivers answers before people have to ask. <b>The agent speaks up</b>: less searching, less uncertainty, better decisions:", "ch": ["“18 hours remaining.”", "“Priority changed this morning.”", "“Budget overrun risk detected.”"], "pts": [["Ask Lumen", "One question, many sources. Lumen searches chats, documents, emails and files, links the information and answers with the sources to check, like “What do I need to know before today's call?”"], ["Projects at a glance", "A dashboard with the day's priorities, project status, your tasks and risk signals, such as a budget at 92% with three weeks left."], ["Workflow and decisions", "Workflow tracking shows what is in progress, waiting or blocked, while a decision log records priority changes and a timeline estimates iterations."], ["Workload", "A workload view warns of overload months and lets Lumen help resolve it."]], "q": "“Not to work harder, but to work with clarity, in a smoother way.”"}]}};
